  m_Name: Module6
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: Assembly-CSharp::PhotoTextContentData
  data:
  - contentName: 0
    modelName: 0
    display: []
    titleName: 'Introduction

'
    description: Basically, the main task is how to keep the computer system in good
      working condition. However, on a larger scale, it has multiple varieties of
      activities. There are three main categories of maintenance.
    has3dView: 0
  - contentName: 0
    modelName: 0
    display: []
    titleName: Categories of Maintenance
    description: "1.Preventive Maintenance \u2013 consist of avoiding the appearance
      of the problems before it can obviously affect the regular operation of the
      computer. This requires regular check-up by scheduling task\r\nThis type of
      computer maintenance therefore concerns both antivirus software and software
      updates. It also includes firewall management, temporary file cleaning and
      hard disk defragmentation.\r\n2.Corrective Maintenance \u2013 means identifying,
      isolating and rectifying faulty parts to repair or replace the equipment or
      software and retore to its operational condition.\r\n3.Evolutionary Maintenance
      \u2013 consists of supporting the computer equipment over time. It may involve
      offering new hardware or software tools to users based on the latest technological
      developments. Evolutionary maintenance also includes system updates and upgrades
      of the computer equipment.\r\n"
    has3dView: 0
  - contentName: 0
    modelName: 0
    display: []
    titleName: 'Software Maintenance

'
    description: 'Software maintenance is the process of modifying and updating software
      systems to keep them functioning correctly, improve their performance, and
      adapt them to changing requirements.

      Hence, you need to verify that
      installed software is current and follow the policies of the organization when
      installing security updates, operating system, and program updates.

      

      Create
      a software maintenance schedule to:

      Review and install the appropriate
      security, software, and driver updates.

      Update the virus definition
      files and scan for viruses and spyware.

      Remove unwanted or unused programs.

      Scan
      hard drives for errors and defragment hard drives.

'
    has3dView: 0
  - contentName: 0
    modelName: 0
    display: []
    titleName: 'Defragmentation

'
    description: "Fragmentation causes your hard disk to work harder, slowing down
      your computer. Removable storage devices like USB flash drives can also become
      fragmented. Windows\u2019 Disk Defragmenter reorganizes fragmented data, allowing
      your disks and drives to work more efficiently. While Disk Defragmenter runs
      on a schedule, you can also manually analyze and defragment your disks and
      drives by following these steps:\r\n\r\nSteps to defragment your hard disk\r\n1.Open
      Disk Defragmenter by clicking the Start button icon. In the search box, type
      Disk Defragmenter, and then, in the list of results, click Disk Defragmenter.\r\n2.Under
      Current status, select the disk you want to defragment.\r\n\nNote: Disk Defragmenter
      might take from several minutes to a few hours to finish, depending on the
      size and degree of fragmentation of your hard disk. You can still use your
      computer during the defragmentation process.\r\nDefragmentation is not recommended
      if you are already using SSD (Solid-State Drive)\r\n"
    has3dView: 0
  - contentName: 0
    modelName: 0
    display: []
    titleName: 'Check Disk

'
    description: "Check Disk \u2013 (Chkdsk) is a command-line utility in Windows
      that scans and repairs errors on hard drives and file systems. It checks for
      issues like bad sectors, lost clusters, and directory errors, and can fix them
      to ensure the drive operates smoothly.\r\n\r\nCHKDSK can fix two types of disk
      errors: logical errors and physical errors. Based on the disk error types,
      you can use the different CHKDSK commands and\r\nswitches to fix hard drive
      errors. CHKDSK /F, CHKDSK /R, and CHKDSK /X are the most used CHKDSK commands.\r\n\r\nSyntax:
      chkdsk volume: /f /v /r /x /I /c\r\nExample: chkdsk C: /f /v /r\r\n\r\nWhere:\r\nVolume
      \u2013 specifies the drive letter (followed by a colon), mount point, or volume
      name.\r\n/F - Check for disk errors, not bad sectors.\r\n/V - on FAT/FAT32:
      Display the Full path and name of every file on the disk.\r\nOn NTFS: Displays
      cleanup messages if any.\r\n/R - Locate the bad sectors on the target hard
      disk and try to recover the readable information from the bad sectors (implies
      /F).\r\n/X - Forces the volume to dismount first, if necessary. It also includes
      the functionality of /f.\r\n/I - NRFS ony: Performs a less vigorous check of
      index entries.\r\n/C - NTFS only: Skips checking of cycles within the folder
      structure.\r\n/B - NTFS only: Re-evaluate bad clusters on the volume <implies
      /R>\r\nNote: to run CHKDSK, click Start button then type CMD (Command Prompt)
      then Command Prompt window will appear. Then type CHKDSK command with proper
      syntax.\r\n"
    has3dView: 0
  - contentName: 0
    modelName: 0
    display: []
    titleName: 'UNINSTALLING UNNECESSARY APPLICATIONS

'
    description: "Why should you uninstall unused software from your computer?\r\nUninstalling
      unused software helps free up valuable storage space on your computer\u2019s
      hard drive.\r\nIt can also improve system performance by reducing background
      processes and unnecessary software dependencies.\r\nAdditionally, removing
      unused software reduces the risk of security vulnerabilities associated with
      outdated or unpatched programs.\r\n"
    has3dView: 0
  - contentName: 0
    modelName: 0
    display: []
    titleName: 'DISABLING STARTUP APPLICATIONS

'
    description: 'Startup Programs are programs, applications and services that can
      automatically launch after you boot up your computer. Some startup programs
      like Cortana, Microsoft OneDrive, UpdaterStartupUtility, and more usually work
      in the background. Third-Party software can also be set at the startup boot.
      You can set your frequently used programs as startup programs.

      

      Startup
      Programs that are Safe to Disable on Windows 10/11:

      1.Cortana

      2.Microsoft
      Edge

      3.Google Chrome

      4.Microsoft Team

      5.Chat

      6.Zoom

      7.Skype

      8.iTunes
      Mobile Helper/iTuneHelper

      9.QuickTime

      10.Adobe Reader

      11.Microsoft
      Office

      12.Xbox App Services

'
    has3dView: 0
  - contentName: 0
    modelName: 0
    display: []
    titleName: 'Startup Programs that should be Enabled on Windows 10/11

'
    description: 'Your antivirus software no matter whether it is the Windows built-in
      tool or third-party software you have downloaded and installed.

      Services,
      drivers, and applications for audio, wireless devices and touchpads (for laptops).

      Intel,
      AMD, and Nvidia graphic card services (like Realtek HD Audio Universal Service)
      and display drivers.

      Automatic data backup software like Dropbox, SugarSync,
      Google Drive, Microsoft OneDrive, etc.

'
    has3dView: 0
  - contentName: 0
    modelName: 0
    display: []
    titleName: "Disabling Startup Applications \u2013There are many reasons why we
      need to disable startup programs in Windows 10 or 11."
    description: "Improve Boot time \u2013 Many startup programs run in the background,
      consuming system resources and slowing down your computer\u2019s boot time.
      Disabling unnecessary programs can significantly reduce the time it takes for
      Windows to start up.\r\nIncrease Performance \u2013 Even after booting, unnecessary
      startup programs can continue to use resources, leading to slower performance
      and potentially impacting other running applications. Disabling these programs
      can free up memory and processing power for your primary tasks.\r\nReduce Source
      Usage \u2013 Some startup programs might be resource-intensive, using a large
      amount of memory, CPU, or internet bandwidth. Disabling these programs can
      help reduce overall resource usage, leading to a smoother and more responsive
      system.\r\nPrevent conflicts: Occasionally, startup programs can conflict with
      other software or hardware, causing issues like crashes, freezes, or errors.
      Disabling problematic programs can help resolve these conflicts.\r\nEnhance
      battery life: Startup programs can drain battery life, especially on laptops.
      By disabling unnecessary programs, you can extend the battery life of your
      device.\r\nImprove security: Some startup programs might be malicious or contain
      vulnerabilities that could compromise your system\u2019s security. Disabling
      suspicious programs can help protect your computer from potential threats.\r\n"
    has3dView: 0
  - contentName: 0
    modelName: 0
    display: []
    titleName: '3 Ways to Disable Startup Programs in Windows 11

'
    description: 'Disable Startup Programs using the Settings App

      1. In Windows,
      launch the Settings app.

      2. On the left side of Settings, choose Apps
      and Select the Startup navigation option.

      3. To disable any startup
      apps that are now active, click the toggle switches next to them.

      Disable
      all Starup Programs using the MSConfig App

      1. Open the Run window by
      pressing Win + R on the keyboard.

      2. In the Run text box, type msconfig.

      3.
      To open MSConfig, click the OK button.

      4. To disable this feature,
      uncheck the Load startup items checkbox on the General tab.

      5. To save
      the updated boot settings, click Apply and select the OK button.

      6.
      On the dialogue box prompt, click Restart.

      Disable Startup Programs
      using the Task Manager App

      1. In Windows, launch the Task Manager app.

      2.
      Navigate to the Startup tab.

      3. Click the Disable option after right-clicking
      on the enabled application.

'
    has3dView: 0
  - contentName: 0
    modelName: 0
    display: []
    titleName: 'Organizing files/folders

'
    description: "How organized are you? This is a crucial aspect to consider when
      using a computer. Organizing files and folders on your computer is similar
      to arranging your clothes in a closet or tidying up your home. If you're good
      at keeping things organized, you\u2019ll find it easy to apply those same skills
      to managing your computer files.\r\n\r\nFive Tips to organize your files and
      folders\r\n1.Establish a Clear hierarchical folder structure \u2013 start your
      files by creating a logical, hierarchical folder structure. The best folder
      structure will mimic the way you work.\r\nExample: \r\n2.Use a consistent naming
      convention \u2013\r\nGive your folders and files specific, logical names\u2014and
      be consistent. The goal is to use names that clearly indicate what's inside
      without having to open it. While there's no one-size-fits-all approach to naming
      conventions, here are some tips to keep in mind.\r\nInclude Keywords. Consider
      what terms you might search for to retrieve the file. And if you are sharing
      the file with a client, consider using words that make it clear what\u2019s
      inside.\r\nAdd a date. By putting a date (e.g. yymmdd) at the beginning of
      your filename, it\u2019ll automatically be listed chronological order.\r\nInclude
      the version. If you\u2019re working with multiple versions of a file, include
      the version number (e.g. Ver4). This will make it easier to identify the most
      recent version and avoid any costly mixups if there are multiple iterations
      of the same file.\r\nSequential Numbers. To arrange your files in a specific
      order, add leading zeros (e.g. 01, 02, and 03) instead of 1, 2, and 3.\r\nKeep
      it concise. Some software programs have character limits on filenames, or don\u2019t
      allow certain special characters (e.g. #, @, and &). To keep your file and
      folder names consistent \u2013 regardless of the program you\u2019re using
      \u2013 include only necessary information and cut anything spare.\r\n3.Add
      Tags \u2013 Depending on the number of files you need to organize, you can
      use a tagging system instead of, or in addition to folder structures. Example,
      if you are an event photographer, you might tag your photos based on events
      (e.g. graduation, birthday2024, Opening_Prog_2024)\r\n4.Delete and archive
      unnecessary files. \u2013 you might consider every month to have time in organizing
      your files and folders. Make your important files easily accessible by creating
      shortcut link on your desktop while deleting those files that are not needed
      and archive those you don\u2019t need right away in external drive or in the
      cloud drive.\r\n5.File as you go - If you work with a lot of files, organizing
      your folders once a month may result in an insurmountable pile of chaos. To
      prevent this, give every file an accurate name and home as soon as you create
      it.\r\n\r\n\r\nNote: Creating a separate drive for your personal files is a
      smart practice. Instead of saving everything on Drive C:, which holds your
      system files, you can create and use a partition like Drive D: for your personal
      data. This way, if your computer gets a virus or the system crashes and Drive
      C: needs to be formatted, your files on Drive D: are more likely to remain
      safe. This setup offers better protection for your important files.\r\n"
    has3dView: 0
